UR FINKEL - the Royal Game of Ur for the Commodore Plus/4
=========================================================

Build @DATE@.  The date is also compiled into the program and drawn on its
menu, so this collection can be identified from the machine itself.

This archive holds:

    urfinkel.prg    the program alone
    urfinkel.d64    a disk image holding the same program
    index.html      the play page, pointed at the .prg next to it
    start-html.sh   serves this folder and opens that page
    src/            the C and 6502 assembly the program was built from
    LICENCE         MIT - it covers the source in src/ as well
    RUNNING.txt     this file

Either binary plays the whole game.  Which one you want depends on what you
are running it on.


1.  A DESKTOP EMULATOR - use urfinkel.prg
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

VICE is the usual one.  Drag urfinkel.prg onto the xplus4 window, or from a
terminal:

    xplus4 urfinkel.prg

YAPE and plus4emu take it the same way.  The .prg starts faster than the
disk image because there is no directory to read first.


2.  A FLOPPY EMULATOR, OR REAL HARDWARE - use urfinkel.d64
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Put urfinkel.d64 on an SD2IEC, a Pi1541 or whatever stands in for the drive,
mount it as you would any disk, and on the machine type:

    dload"urfinkel"
    run

DLOAD"*" works too - the program is the first thing in the directory, which
is the shortest thing to type.

The image is a 1541 .d64 rather than a .d81 because every drive and every
emulator reads one without being told anything about it.

It has been run this way on a restored PAL Plus/4.


3.  IN A BROWSER, FROM THIS FOLDER - use start-html.sh
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ./start-html.sh

It serves this folder on a free local port and opens index.html in your
browser.  That page boots a Plus/4 and loads the urfinkel.prg sitting next
to it.  Press Ctrl-C in the terminal to stop the server.

IT HAS TO BE SERVED, not opened by double-clicking.  A page opened as a
file:// URL is not allowed to read urfinkel.prg off your disk, so the
emulator would come up and then sit there with nothing to run.  Serving the
folder over http://localhost is what makes that read legal, and it is the
only thing the script is for.  It needs python3, which macOS and every
Linux already have.

ONE THING STILL COMES FROM THE INTERNET.  The emulator itself - EmulatorJS,
which is not part of this project and carries its own licence - is fetched
from its CDN when the page loads.  The game is local; the machine it runs
on is not.  With no connection, use VICE and the .prg instead, which needs
nothing but the two files in front of you.

The same page is hosted, if you would rather not run anything:

    https://vonglurt.github.io/urfinkel/


CHECKING THIS ARCHIVE
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every file published, with its size, its MD5 and its SHA-256, and the exact
compiler and command line that produced the binaries:

    https://github.com/vonglurt/urfinkel/blob/main/build/CHECKSUMS.txt

Each hash is also published on its own, in the form the checking tools
expect - urfinkel.prg.sha256, urfinkel.d64.md5 and so on.  Save one beside
the file it names and run:

    shasum -a 256 -c urfinkel.prg.sha256
    md5sum -c urfinkel.prg.md5


BUILDING IT YOURSELF
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

src/ holds everything the program is made of - the C, the headers, and the
two assembly files (blit.s, the blitter; irq.s, the raster interrupt) that
the rest is linked against.

You need cc65, which gives you cl65:

    brew install cc65          # macOS
    apt install cc65           # Debian, Ubuntu

Then, from the folder holding src/, this exact command rebuilds
urfinkel.prg - it is the one that produced the copy in this archive, and
the build date is passed in rather than taken from today's clock so that
what comes out is byte-for-byte the same file:

@COMPILE@

And to pack it back into a disk image, with c1541 from VICE:

@DISKIMAGE@

The output is named urfinkel-rebuilt.prg rather than urfinkel.prg so that
the file you started with survives to be compared against:

    cmp urfinkel-rebuilt.prg urfinkel.prg && echo identical

That should report nothing but "identical".  If it does not, the compiler
version is the first thing to check - see CHECKSUMS.txt on GitHub, which
records the exact cl65 this was built with.

The full project - the Makefile, the tests, the reports, the history - is
at:

    https://github.com/vonglurt/urfinkel

MIT licensed; see LICENCE, which covers the source here as well as the
binaries.
